From: Try Omarchy contributors
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: leave the Mac's brightness keys with macOS

Applies after the Try Omarchy full-grab-reenable patch.

Full grab installs an HID-level CGEventTap so that system shortcuts reach
the focused guest before macOS acts on them. handleEventLocked reports
every key event as consumed once the keyboard is captured, mapped or not,
so the tap swallows everything it is offered.

That includes the display brightness keys. In its default mode an Apple
keyboard sends them as ordinary key events with dedicated keycodes (144
and 145) rather than as F1/F2. Neither has a guest mapping, so while the
Omarchy window is focused the guest receives nothing useful and macOS
receives nothing at all: the screen cannot be dimmed or brightened without
first clicking away from the VM.

Recognise those keycodes and hand the events straight back to macOS from
the tap, before the conversion that would offer them to the view, and have
the window path decline them as well so nothing leaks to the guest when
macOS also delivers the key to our key window.

Testing by keycode keeps the change narrow. With "Use F1, F2, etc. as
standard function keys" enabled the same caps arrive as F1/F2, which are
not in the set and continue to reach the guest as function keys. Volume
and media-transport keys are a different event type the tap never
captured, so they are unaffected.

--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -358,10 +358,28 @@
 - (void) raiseAllKeys;
 @end
 
 QemuCocoaView *cocoaView;
 
+/*
+ * The Mac's display brightness keys. In its default mode an Apple keyboard
+ * sends these as plain key events with dedicated keycodes, not as F1/F2,
+ * so a keycode test leaves real function keys alone: with "Use F1, F2,
+ * etc. as standard function keys" enabled the same caps arrive as F1/F2
+ * and still reach the guest.
+ */
+static bool cocoa_is_host_brightness_key(CGKeyCode keycode)
+{
+    switch (keycode) {
+    case 144: /* brightness up */
+    case 145: /* brightness down */
+        return true;
+    default:
+        return false;
+    }
+}
+
 static CGEventRef handleTapEvent(CGEventTapProxy proxy, CGEventType type, CGEventRef cgEvent, void *userInfo)
 {
     QemuCocoaView *view = userInfo;
 
     /*
@@ -376,10 +394,21 @@
         type == kCGEventTapDisabledByUserInput) {
         [view reenableEventTap];
         return cgEvent;
     }
 
+    /*
+     * The tap sees the Mac's display brightness keys before macOS does,
+     * and the capture below would swallow them for the whole session. The
+     * guest has no use for them; hand them straight back to macOS.
+     */
+    if ((type == kCGEventKeyDown || type == kCGEventKeyUp) &&
+        cocoa_is_host_brightness_key(CGEventGetIntegerValueField(cgEvent,
+                                    kCGKeyboardEventKeycode))) {
+        return cgEvent;
+    }
+
     NSEvent *event = [NSEvent eventWithCGEvent:cgEvent];
     if ([view isKeyboardCaptured] && [view handleEvent:event]) {
         COCOA_DEBUG("Global events tap: qemu handled the event, capturing!\n");
         return NULL;
     }
@@ -1075,10 +1104,16 @@
             }
             return true;
         case NSEventTypeKeyDown:
             keycode = cocoa_keycode_to_linux([event keyCode]);
 
+            // The Mac's brightness keys stay with macOS even when they
+            // reach our key window rather than the tap.
+            if (cocoa_is_host_brightness_key([event keyCode])) {
+                return false;
+            }
+
             // Keep Command chords with the host unless the focused guest
             // captured the keyboard.
             if (![self isKeyboardCaptured] &&
                 ([event modifierFlags] & NSEventModifierFlagCommand)) {
                 return false;
@@ -1113,10 +1148,14 @@
             }
             return true;
         case NSEventTypeKeyUp:
             keycode = cocoa_keycode_to_linux([event keyCode]);
 
+            if (cocoa_is_host_brightness_key([event keyCode])) {
+                return false;
+            }
+
             // don't pass the guest a spurious key-up if we treated this
             // command-key combo as a host UI action
             if (![self isKeyboardCaptured] &&
                 ([event modifierFlags] & NSEventModifierFlagCommand)) {
                 return true;
